ransitive triple.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCDMTCS\nResearc\nh\nRep\nort\nSeries\nSearc\nh\nand\nEn\nume (https://www.cs.auckland.ac.nz/research/groups/CDMTCS/researchreports/085denny.pdf)\nciteturn0search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; A (v,k,l)-Mendelsohn design is a set V, together with a collection ... to itself, the design is said to be self-converse.\nCase Studies\nChapter 6\nDefinition:\nA (v,k,l)-Mendelsohn design is a set V, together with a collection\nB of ordered k-tuples of distinct elements of V, called blocks, such\nthat each ordered pair (x,y), with x ¹ y, is contained in exactly l\nblocks.  A (v,3,l)-Mendelsohn design is a Mendelsohn \ntriple\nsystem, denoted MTS(v,l).\nThe converse of an MTS(v,l) is the design obtained by reversing\nthe order of its blocks.  If the converse of a given MTS is isomorphic\nto itself, the design is said to be self-converse.\nThe underlying \ndesign of an MTS(v,l) is the 2-(v,k,2l) BIBD\nwhich results from ignoring the ordering structure of the blocks.\nA (v,k,2l) BIBD is orientable if and only if a cyclic order can be\nplaced on its blocks to form a valid MTS(v,l).\n6.5.2  CURRENT RESULTS\nTwo MTSs are equivalent if they are isomorphic or if one is isomorphic to the converse of\nthe other.  Prior to the work carried out in this case study, the number, M(v), of inequivalent\nMTS(v,1) had been computed [30, 31] for orders v £ 10, as summarised in the table below:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCanad. Math. Bull. Vol. 59 (1), 2016 pp. 36–49 (https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/106E422129090B2532AA84BB7234360E/S0008439500019603a.pdf/distributive_and_antidistributive_mendelsohn_triple_systems.pdf)\nciteturn0search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Distributive and Anti-distributive Mendelsohn Triple Systems ... triple system (resp. quasigroup) is not necessarily self-converse, i.e., isomorphic to its\nAn explicit description of the quasigroups of order 81 can be found in [18, Ex-\nample 3.4]. Te GAP code used for the calculations is available on our website2.\nGiven an MTS(v), (V, B), its converse is the MTS(v), (V, B′), obtained by writing\nall the blocks in the reverse order. In terms of the associated quasigroups, Q = (V, ○)\nand Q′ = (V, ○′), respectively, Q′ is the converse of Q, i.e., x○′y = y○x. A Mendelsohn\ntriple system (resp. quasigroup) is not necessarily self-converse, i.e., isomorphic to its\nconverse.\nProposition 2.13\nLet (V, B) be a Mendelsohn triple system such that the associated\nquasigroup Q = Aﬀ(G, k) is distributive. Ten (V, B) is self-converse if and only if k\nand I −k are conjugate in Aut(G).\nProof\nTe converse of Q is the quasigroup Q′ = Aﬀ(G, I −k). According to Propo-\nsition 2.11, Q is isomorphic to Q′ if and only if there is an automorphism ψ ∈Aut(G)\nsuch that I −k = ψkψ−1, i.e., if and only if k and I −k are conjugate in Aut(G).\nFor example, if G is a cyclic group, then Aut(G) is commutative, hence Q is self-\nconverse if and only if k = I −k, i.e., if and only if Q is a Steiner quasigroup. In par-\nticular, a proper distributive MTS(p) where p is prime is never self-converse. Hence,\nas a(p) = 2, the systems are the converse of each other.\n2http://www.karlin.mff.cuni.cz/~{}stanovsk/quandles\nhttps://doi.org/10.4153/CMB-2015-053-2 Published online by Cambridge University Press--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe spectrum for self-converse large set of pure Mendelsohn triple systems (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0012365X21003320)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.9 years ago; Crawled: last week; # The spectrum for self-converse large set of pure Mendelsohn triple systems☆\n\n## Discrete Mathematics\n\nDate: December 2021\n\nArticle: 112619\n\nVolume: Volume 344, Issue 12\n\nView accessibility information\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\nResearch article\n\nOpen archive\n\nUnder an Elsevier user license\n\nGet rights and content\n\n# The spectrum for self-converse large set of pure Mendelsohn triple systems☆\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Yuanyuan Liu ^{a}, Hongtao Zhao ^{b}, Henry Zhou ^{c}\n\nShow more\n\nView PDF Download full issue\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.disc.2021.112619\n\n## Abstract\n\nAn $LPMTS(v,\\lambda )$ is a collection of $\\frac{v-2}{\\lambda }$ disjoint pure Mendelsohn triple system $PMTS(v,\\lambda )s$ on the same set of v elements. An $LPMT{S}^{⁎}(v)$ is a special $LPMTS(v,1)$ which contains exactly $\\frac{v-2}{2}$ converse pairs of $PMTS(v)s$. In this paper, we mainly discuss the existence of an $LPMT{S}^{⁎}(v)$ for $v\\equiv 6,10\\phantom{\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}\\mathrm{mod 12}$ and get the following conclusions: (1) there exists an $LPMT{S}^{⁎}(v)$ if and only if $v\\equiv 0,4\\phantom{\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}\\mathrm{mod 6},\\phantom{\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}v\\ge 4$ and $v\\ne 6$. (2) There exists an $LPMTS(v,\\lambda )$ with index $\\lambda \\equiv 2,4\\phantom{\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}\\mathrm{mod 6}$ if and only if $v\\equiv 0,4\\phantom{\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}\\mathrm{mod 6},v\\ge 2\\lambda +2,v\\equiv 2\\phantom{\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}\\mathrm{mod}\\phantom{\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}\\mathit{\\lambda }$.\n\n## Keywords\n\nLarge set\n\n;\n\nPure\n\n;\n\nSelf-converse\n\n;\n\nMendelsohn triple system\n\n^{☆}\n    \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQuasi-(λ; n)-distance-balanced graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.02634)\nciteturn0academia17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.2 years ago; In this paper, we introduce quasi-({\\lambda}; n)-distance-balanced graphs and then study some properties of these graphs and present a formula to construct such graphs for arbitrarily diameter d. ... Some problems and conjecture are involved.\nTitle: Quasi-(λ; n)-distance-balanced graphs\nAuthors: Ehsan Pourhadi, Morteza Faghani\nDate: Thu Sep  5 21:09:55 2019\n\nFor every pair of vertices u and v with d(u; v) = n, Wun G v denotes the set of all vertices of G that are closer to u than to v. In this paper, we introduce quasi-({\\lambda}; n)-distance-balanced graphs and then study some properties of these graphs and present a formula to construct such graphs for arbitrarily diameter d. For n = 1, this class of graphs contains the quasi-{\\lambda}-DB graphs recently introduced by Abedi et al. [Quasi-{\\lambda}-distance-balanced graphs, Discrete Appl. Math. 227 (2017) 21{28]. Moreover, we will take a look at the problems arisen by Abedi et al. Some problems and conjecture are involved.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHypercube Graph (https://mathworld.wolfram.com/HypercubeGraph.html)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; 3  | cubical graph Image: Q_3\n3  | cubical graph Image: Q_3\n4  | tesseract graph Image: Q_4\n\nFor Image: n>=2, hypercube graphs are Hamiltonian, and any Hamiltonian cycle of a labeled hypercube graph defines a cyclic binary Gray code (Skiena 1990, p. 149; Mütze 2024). The numbers of Hamiltonian cycles in hypercube graphs were considered by Dixon and Goodman (1975).\n\nHypercube graphs are also graceful (Maheo 1980, Kotzig 1981, Gallian 2025), antipodal, and (most likely) dominating unique.\n\nThe numbers of (directed) Hamiltonian paths whose endpoints are nonadjacent on an Image: n-hypercube graph for Image: n=1, 2, ... are 0, 0, 48, 48384, 129480729600, ... (OEIS A006070; extending the result of Gardner 1986, pp. 23-24). The numbers of (directed) Hamiltonian cycles for Image: n=2, 3, ... are 2, 12, 2688, 1813091520, ... (Harary et al. 1988; OEIS A003042). For Image: n=1, the graph Image: Q_1=K_2 has no Hamiltonian cycle under the convention that a graph cycle has at least three vertices.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nON SOME PROPERTIES of QUASI-DISTANCE-BALANCED GRAPHS (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/322800453_ON_SOME_PROPERTIES_of_QUASI-DISTANCE-BALANCED_GRAPHS)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; We disprove a conjecture regarding characterization of strongly distance-balanced graphs posed by Balakrishnan et al. (2009) by providing infinitely many counterexamples, and answer a question posed by Kutnar et al. in (2006) regarding the existence of semisymmetric distance-balanced graphs which are not strongly distance-balanced by providing an infinite family of such examples. ... This paper introduces the concept of quasi- -distance-balanced graphs, and gives some interesting examples and constructions.\nArticle PDF Available\n\n# ON SOME PROPERTIES of QUASI-DISTANCE-BALANCED GRAPHS\n\n  * January 2018\n  * Bulletin of the Australian Mathematical Society 97(2):1-8\n\nDOI:10.1017/S000497271700096X\n\nAuthors:\n\nAdemir Hujdurović\n\n  * University of Primorska\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract and Figures\n\nFor an edge uv in a graph G , $W_{u,v}^{G}$ denotes the set of all vertices of G that are closer to u than to v . A graph G is said to be quasi-distance-balanced if there exists a constant \\unicode[STIX]{x1D706}>1 such that |W_{u,v}^{G}|=\\unicode[STIX]{x1D706}^{\\pm 1}|W_{v,u}^{G}| for every pair of adjacent vertices u and v . The existence of nonbipartite quasi-distance-balanced graphs is an open problem. In this paper we investigate the possible structure of cycles in quasi-distance-balanced graphs and generalise the previously known result that every quasi-distance-balanced graph is triangle-free. the family of generalized Petersen graphs was studied in [11, 14]. For more results on this and\n\nrelated concepts see [2, 4, 8, 12, 13].\n\nQuasi-distance-balanced graphs, introduced by Abedi et al. in [1] generalize the concept\n\nof distance-balanced graphs. A graph G is said to be quasi-distance-balanced if there exists a\n\npositive rational number λ > 1, such that for any edge uv of G, either |W u,v|=λ|W v ,u|or\n\n|W v,u|=λ|W u,v|. In this case we denote with QDB(G) = λ. In [1] it is proved that every\n\nquasi-distance-balanced graph is triangle-free and based on that result it is proved that the only\n\nquasi-distance-balanced graphs with diameter two are the complete bipartite graphs. Quasi-\n\ndistance-balanced lexicographic and Cartesian products are characterized, see [1, Theorem 1.4\n\nand Theorem 1.5]. All known examples of quasi-distance-balanced graphs are bipartite, so the\n\nfollowing question, which was posed in [1] is still open.\n\nProblem 1.1. [1, Problem 1.1] Does there exist a non-bipartite quasi-distance-balanced graph?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nList of papers (https://users.fmf.uni-lj.si/klavzar/papers-all.htm)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Revisiting d-distance (independent) domination in trees and in bipartite graphs (with Cs. ... The conjecture on distance-balancedness of generalized Petersen graphs holds when internal edges have jumps 3 or 4 (with G.\n  * 426. S-packing chromatic critical graphs (with G. Boruzanlı Ekinci, Cs. Bujtás, D. Gözüpek), Discrete Appl. Math. 385 (2026) 77-85.   PDF\n  * 425. Three algorithmic approaches to the general position problem (with Z. Hamed-Labbafian, N. Sabeghi, M. Tavakoli), Bull. Aust. Math. Soc. 113 (2026) 1-9.   PDF\n  * 424. Revisiting d-distance (independent) domination in trees and in bipartite graphs (with Cs. Bujtás, V. Iršič Chenoweth, G. Zhang), Discrete Math. 349 (2026) Paper 114972, 11 pp.   PDF\n  * 423. General position problems in strong and lexicographic products of graphs (with P. Dokyeesun, D. Kuziak, J. Tian), Comput. Appl. Math. 45 (2026) Paper 97, 13 pp.   PDF\n  * 422. Builder-Blocker general position games (with J. Tian, J. Tuite), Commun. Comb. Optim. 11 (2026) 465-486.   PDF\n  * 421. Extremal results on k-stepwise irregul